{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfy5FSY3acuLgpnYNZF5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result:** Dq ≈ 505 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 581 cm⁻¹ (Δt = 10Dq ≈ 5050 cm⁻¹).\n\nI did not use the tools. I solved the 3T1 secular problem by hand.\n\n**Approach.** A tetrahedral d⁸ ion has the opposite sign of crystal field to an octahedral d⁸ ion, so I used the octahedral d⁸ expressions with Dq replaced by –Dq. Take Δ = Dq > 0 for the tetrahedral field. The 3F ground term splits into 3T1(F) (ground), 3T2 and 3A2, and 3P becomes 3T1(P).\n\n- The two 3T1 terms mix through a 2×2 matrix, with energies measured from the free-ion 3F term: [[–6Dq, 4B],[4B, 15B + 6Dq]].\n- 3T2 lies at +2Dq on this scale.\n\n**Band assignments.**\n- ν₁ (4400 cm⁻¹) is 3T1(F) → 3T2.\n- ν₃ (15 500 cm⁻¹) is 3T1(F) → 3T1(P), the second 3T1 term, which is how I read the \"3T1(2)\" label.\n\nThe 3A2 band is not given, so it is not used in the fit.\n\n**Solving.**\n1. The two 3T1 eigenvalues are 7.5B ± d, where d = √[(15B + 12Dq)²/4 + 16B²].\n2. The ν₃ band is the splitting of these two eigenvalues, so 2d = 15 500 and d = 7750.\n3. The ν₁ band gives 2Dq – (7.5B – d) = 4400. This simplifies to Dq = 3.75B – 1675.\n4. Substituting into the expression for d gives 916B² – 603 000B + 40 940 000 = 0.\n5. The roots are B ≈ 77 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 581 cm⁻¹. The first is unphysically small, so B ≈ 581 cm⁻¹.\n6. Then Dq = 3.75 × 581 – 1675 ≈ 505 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check.** With these values ν₁ = 1010 + 3389 ≈ 4400 cm⁻¹ and ν₃ = 2 × 7750 = 15 500 cm⁻¹. The spin-allowed triplet manifold of d⁸ does not depend on C, so the assumed C/B = 4.5 does not enter.\n\nB ≈ 581 cm⁻¹ is about 56% of the free-ion value for Ni²⁺, which indicates substantial covalency.\n\n{\"Dq\": 505, \"B\": 581, \"C\": 2616, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Two spin-allowed bands (3T1→3T2 and 3T1→3T1(P)) fix Dq and B in the tetrahedral d8 model; C = 4.5B does not affect these bands.\"}"}],"container